{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WdFEZNbCw8FRxzPUoh4YKK","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/writing_plan.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"meta:\\\\n  venue: CoRL 2026\\\\n  track: main\\\\n  template: docs/paper/corl_2026.sty\\\\n  template_note: \\\\\\\"CoRL 2026 template already configured in project paper directory\\\\\\\"\\\\n  page_limit: 8\\\\n  deadline_abstract: \\\\\\\"2026-05-26\\\\\\\"\\\\n  deadline_paper: \\\\\\\"2026-05-29\\\\\\\"\\\\n  core_claim: >\\\\n    VLA token pruning robustness is governed by two interacting axes —\\\\n    connector architecture (token-independent vs token-entangling, diagnosed\\\\n    by importance CV 0.038→0.314) and task complexity (simple vs multi-step) —\\\\n    with CV determining the compression threshold at which pruning method\\\\n    becomes critical across 5 models × 4 task suites.\\\\n  created_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-22T23:30:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  updated_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-23T00:15:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  update_log: \\\\\\\"D175: Claim 3 upgraded — CV determines method-critical threshold; added fig_method_gap; BART structural property in Discussion\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\npaper_outline:\\\\n  - section: abstract\\\\n    word_budget: 200\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Problem: Vision token pruning accelerates ViT inference with negligible accuracy loss, but whether this transfers to VLA closed-loop control across diverse architectures is untested\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Framework: 2-axis — (1) token coupling (independent vs entangling, connector-determined, diagnosed by CV) × (2) task complexity (simple single-step vs complex multi-step)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Result 1: Token-entangling connectors show task-amplified collapse — X-VLA drops 27.8pp on Goal vs 1.6pp on Object at k=0.9; SmolVLA pixel-shuffle collapses to 0% at 5% removal\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Result 2: CV determines the compression threshold at which method choice becomes critical — low CV (0.055): 15× more method-sensitive than high CV (≥0.15) at k=0.95; at aggressive compression (k≤0.5) random outperforms L2norm by 36pp for low-CV models\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Result 3: VTR recovers 60%+ SR where hard pruning yields 0%, M-curve flat (near-lossless at any compression ratio)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Scale: 5 VLAs (450M–7B), 3 connector families, 4 LIBERO suites\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments, method]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: introduction\\\\n    word_budget: 800\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Hook: Removing 5% of vision tokens crashes one VLA to 0% SR but leaves another at 98% — the difference is not model size but a single architectural choice: the vision connector\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Context: VLAs inherit ViT encoders where token pruning saves 50%+ FLOPs. Growing literature proposes VLA-specific pruning (VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, EfficientVLA) on individual models, implicitly assuming architecture-independent effectiveness\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Gap: No work systematically compares pruning robustness across VLA connector architectures and task distributions. The critical factor determining prunability is unknown\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Insight: 2-axis framework — pruning sensitivity = f(connector coupling × task complexity). Token-independent connectors (linear, MLP) degrade gracefully; token-entangling connectors (pixel-shuffle, BART encoder) catastrophically collapse, amplified by multi-step tasks\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Mechanism: CV of token L2 norms captures coupling on a continuous spectrum (0.038→0.314), predicting tolerance ceiling AND the threshold at which pruning method becomes critical — low-CV models are 15× more method-sensitive than high-CV models at k=0.95\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Contributions: [C1: 2-axis compressibility framework validated across 5 VLAs × 4 suites, C2: CV as zero-cost diagnostic for tolerance + method-critical threshold, C3: VTR learned re-encoding recovers entangled tokens from 0% to baseline]\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments]\\\\n    citation_hints:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA models: π0, OpenVLA, SmolVLA for context\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Token pruning: FastV, ToMe, DynamicViT as ViT pruning context\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA efficiency: VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, EfficientVLA as concurrent work\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LIBERO benchmark\\\\\\\"\\\\n    figures: [fig_teaser]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: related_work\\\\n    word_budget: 500\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 1 — ViT token pruning (DynamicViT, ToMe, EViT): 50-66% removal with <0.5% ImageNet drop, but untested in closed-loop VLA control where spatial information drives actions\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 2 — VLM/VLA token efficiency (FastV, PruMerge, VLA-Cache, VLA-IAP, EfficientVLA, VLA-Pruner): VLM methods exploit attention patterns; VLA-specific works test individual models. None compare across architectures\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 3 — Vision connectors (LLaVA MLP, BLIP-2 Q-Former, pixel-shuffle): connector design shapes downstream token structure, but pruning implications unstudied\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Differentiation: First cross-architecture, cross-task VLA token analysis; our framework explains when and why single-model pruning methods succeed or fail\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [introduction]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: method\\\\n    word_budget: 900\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§3.1 Models and Connectors: 5 VLAs spanning 3 connector families — pure linear (π0 3.3B, π0.5 4B), MLP (OpenVLA 7B), BART encoder (X-VLA 880M), pixel-shuffle (SmolVLA 450M). Table 1: model properties including token count and CV\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§3.2 Pruning Protocol: Post-connector token pruning at retention ratio k ∈ {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95}. Two strategies: L2 norm selection (importance-based, deterministic) and random subsampling (uniform, stochastic). Pruned positions replaced with zero vectors\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§3.3 Compressibility Diagnostic — Importance CV: CV = σ/μ of post-connector token L2 norms. Zero-cost: one forward pass, no pruning required. Continuous predictor ranging from 0.038 (uniform, token-independent) to 0.314 (heterogeneous, token-entangled)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§3.4 Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR): Lightweight cross-attention module (~22M params) that learns to re-encode M output tokens from pruned input. For token-entangling connectors where hard pruning yields 0%\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: []\\\\n    notation_table:\\\\n      k: \\\\\\\"retention ratio (fraction of tokens kept)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      CV: \\\\\\\"coefficient of variation of token L2 norms (σ/μ)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      M: \\\\\\\"number of VTR output tokens\\\\\\\"\\\\n      SR: \\\\\\\"success rate on LIBERO (500 episodes per condition)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    tables: [tab_model_properties]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: experiments\\\\n    word_budget: 2800\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.1 Setup: LIBERO benchmark (4 suites: Object, Spatial, Goal, Long; 10 tasks × 50 episodes each). 4 models evaluated closed-loop (π0.5, X-VLA, OpenVLA, SmolVLA); 2 via offline metrics (π0, OFT). Evaluation: mean SR over 500 episodes with Wilson CI\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.2 2-Axis Framework Overview (Claim 1): Token-independent connectors (π0.5, OpenVLA) degrade gracefully across suites; token-entangling connectors (SmolVLA, X-VLA) show task-dependent collapse. Key evidence: X-VLA Object k=0.9=97.6% vs Goal k=0.9=70.2% (-27.8pp) — same connector, same k, only task differs. Table 2 main results\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.3 Pixel-Shuffle Entanglement (Claim 2): SmolVLA 0% at k=0.95 on all 4 suites × 2 methods. Official + reproduced checkpoints confirm. 5% removal destroys performance regardless of method or task. Spatial folding creates irreversible cross-token dependencies\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - >\\\\n        §4.4 When Does Pruning Method Matter? CV as Sensitivity Predictor (Claim 3, D175 upgraded):\\\\n        CV determines the compression threshold at which pruning method becomes\\\\n        critical. High CV (≥0.15): method-independent at k≥0.9 (X-VLA Spatial\\\\n        Δ=1.2pp). Low CV (0.055): method-critical even at k=0.95 (π0.5 Object\\\\n        Δ=28.8pp) — 15× sensitivity ratio. ⚠️ Caveat: high-CV method-independence\\\\n        breaks at k≤0.5 (X-VLA Random>>L2norm 28-40pp). At aggressive compression\\\\n        (k=0.5), gap(R−L2) scales monotonically: π0.5=+36pp, X-VLA=+27pp,\\\\n        OpenVLA=−1.8pp. Fig: Method Gap at k=0.95 grouped bar chart.\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.5 Cross-Suite Task Complexity (Claim 5): Simple (Object/Spatial) vs Complex (Goal/Long). π0.5 L2 k=0.95: Object −0pp, Spatial −0.2pp, Goal −7.0pp, Long −4.8pp. Task complexity creates 5-7pp additional penalty even for token-independent connectors; entangling connectors amplify dramatically\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.6 VTR Recovery (Claim 4): SmolVLA VTR recovers from 0% to 60.1±1.3% (cross-attention) and 63.4±2.6% (MLP adapter) at M=32, p=0.15 (architecture-independent). M-curve flat: M=16(56.9%)≈M=128(58.0%), near-lossless at any compression ratio. Cross-suite: Spatial 53.2%, Goal 56.4% where pruning yields 0%\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: []\\\\n    registry_refs:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"eval_pi05_l2norm_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"eval_pi05_random_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"eval_xvla_l2norm_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"eval_xvla_random_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"eval_openvla_l2norm_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"eval_openvla_random_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"eval_official_smolvla_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"train_smolvla_sc_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"eval_smolvla_sc_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n    tables: [tab_main_results, tab_vtr_results]\\\\n    figures: [fig_pruning_overview, fig_method_gap]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: discussion\\\\n    word_budget: 300\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Limitations: LIBERO simulation only (no real robot). Models differ in backbone, encoder, and fusion strategy, preventing full causal isolation of connector effect. π0 analyzed offline only. CV correlation spans 3 evaluated models\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Non-monotonicity as structural property: X-VLA Goal exhibits identical non-monotonicity in both L2norm and random pruning (Δ=0.2pp), confirming this is a structural property of the BART encoder's position sensitivity, not an artifact of any particular token scoring criterion (D173-D175)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"π0.5 random cliff at k=0.85–0.95 (hypergeometric model RMSE=0.025 captures onset, Appendix); V-shape recovery at k≤0.7 unexplained\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Small-N regime: X-VLA has only 50 tokens — removing 3 tokens at k=0.95 already catastrophic on Goal. Absolute token budget interacts with CV\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Design implications: Practitioners should compute CV before deploying token pruning; pixel-shuffle VLAs require learned re-encoding; task complexity must be factored into pruning budget\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: conclusion\\\\n    word_budget: 200\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Summary: 2-axis framework (connector coupling × task complexity) explains VLA token pruning robustness across 5 models (450M–7B) × 4 LIBERO suites\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"CV provides zero-cost diagnostic predicting both tolerance and the method-critical threshold from a single forward pass\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VTR recovers entangled tokens from 0% to near-baseline at any compression ratio\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Findings inform principled deployment of token pruning in VLA systems\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments, discussion]\\\\n\\\\nvisualization_plan:\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_teaser\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: >\\\\n      2-axis framework teaser (multi-panel). Panel (a): Conceptual 2×2 matrix\\\\n      showing token-independent/entangling × simple/complex with model positions\\\\n      and observed behaviors. Panel (b): X-VLA pruning curves for Object vs Goal\\\\n      suites, demonstrating task-complexity amplification at same k.\\\\n    narrative_intent: >\\\\n      Pruning robustness is governed by TWO interacting axes — connector\\\\n      architecture AND task complexity — and the interaction is dramatic:\\\\n      X-VLA drops 1.6pp on Object but 27.8pp on Goal at the same k=0.9.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Multi-panel: (a) conceptual diagram + (b) statistical curves\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Panel (a): 2×2 grid with axes labeled 'Token Coupling' (independent→entangling) and 'Task Complexity' (simple→complex). Place model names in cells with SR annotations. Color gradient green→red for severity\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Panel (b): X-VLA SR vs k (0.5→1.0) for Object (blue-ish) and Goal (red-ish). Shade the gap at k=0.9 to emphasize 27.8pp. Add baseline dashed lines\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern F: direct annotation on key data points (97.6% vs 70.2%)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Full textwidth, 2 subplots side by side\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: eval_xvla_l2norm_* (Object + Goal suites)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: introduction\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"2:1\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_pruning_overview\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: >\\\\n      Pruning degradation overview: SR vs retention ratio k for all 4 evaluated\\\\n      models on LIBERO-Object (L2 norm). Shows the full spectrum from tolerant\\\\n      (π0.5 plateau) to catastrophic (SmolVLA 0% flatline).\\\\n    narrative_intent: >\\\\n      Four VLAs span a complete degradation spectrum determined by their\\\\n      connector: π0.5 maintains 98% through k=0.85, OpenVLA degrades linearly,\\\\n      X-VLA cliffs at k=0.7, SmolVLA collapses at k=0.95 — confirming\\\\n      connector architecture as the primary axis.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Line plot: k (x-axis, 0.3→1.0) vs SR% (y-axis, 0→100)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"4 curves with distinct colors and markers per model: π0.5=blue circle, OpenVLA=green square, X-VLA=orange triangle, SmolVLA=red X\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern A: light gray regime bands separating 'safe' (k≥0.9) and 'aggressive' (k≤0.5) zones\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern F: annotate SmolVLA 0% flatline, X-VLA cliff at k=0.7, π0.5 plateau\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dashed horizontal lines for each model's baseline\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Include CV value in legend: 'π0.5 (CV=0.055)' etc.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: eval_pi05_l2norm_*_object, eval_xvla_l2norm_*_object, eval_openvla_l2norm_*_object, eval_official_smolvla_*_object\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_method_gap\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: >\\\\n      Method Gap at k=0.95 (D175 Reviewer design). Panel A: Grouped bar chart —\\\\n      x-axis: model/suite combinations, y-axis: |SR(Random) - SR(L2norm)| in pp.\\\\n      π0.5 bars 21-29pp (red/warm), all others ≤1.4pp (blue/cool). One-glance\\\\n      evidence that CV determines method sensitivity. Panel B (supplementary or\\\\n      inset): X-VLA Goal L2norm (solid) + Random (dashed) curves, k≥0.9 region\\\\n      shaded showing near-complete overlap (Δ≤1.4pp), annotated.\\\\n    narrative_intent: >\\\\n      CV determines the compression threshold at which pruning method becomes\\\\n      critical: π0.5 (CV=0.055) shows 15× larger method gap at k=0.95 than\\\\n      X-VLA (CV=0.151), establishing CV as a predictor of method sensitivity,\\\\n      not just tolerance.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Panel A: grouped bars, model/suite on x-axis, |Δ(R-L2)| on y-axis\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Color-code by magnitude: π0.5 bars warm/red (large gap), others cool/blue (small gap)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Horizontal dashed line at ~2pp as 'method-independent' reference\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Direct value annotation on each bar\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Panel B (if space): X-VLA Goal curves, L2norm solid + Random dashed, nearly overlapping at k≥0.9\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern F: annotate 'Δ ≤ 1.4pp' in overlap region\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"If tight on space: Panel B → appendix, Panel A standalone\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: >\\\\n      registry: eval_pi05_l2norm_k095_*, eval_pi05_random_k095_*,\\\\n      eval_xvla_l2norm_k095_*, eval_xvla_random_k095_*,\\\\n      eval_xvla_l2norm_*_goal, eval_xvla_random_*_goal\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\ntable_plan:\\\\n  - table_id: tab_model_properties\\\\n    caption: >\\\\n      VLA models studied. Token coupling type is determined by the vision\\\\n      connector architecture. CV = coefficient of variation of token L2 norms\\\\n      (one forward pass). Evaluated = closed-loop LIBERO; Analyzed = offline\\\\n      metrics or literature only.\\\\n    placement: method\\\\n    type: configuration\\\\n    columns: [Model, Params, Connector, Tokens, CV, Coupling, Eval]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"manual\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >\\\\n      7 rows: π0 (3.3B, linear, 256, 0.038, independent, offline),\\\\n      π0.5 (4B, linear, 768, 0.055, independent, closed-loop),\\\\n      X-VLA (880M, linear+BART 12L, 50, 0.151, entangling, closed-loop),\\\\n      OpenVLA (7B, MLP 3L, 256, 0.215, independent, closed-loop),\\\\n      OFT (7B, MLP 3L, 512, 0.219, independent, offline),\\\\n      SmolVLA (450M, pixel-shuffle, 128, 0.314, entangling, closed-loop).\\\\n      Horizontal rule between closed-loop and offline-only models.\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_main_results\\\\n    caption: >\\\\n      Pruning results on LIBERO (success rate %, 500 episodes each).\\\\n      L2 = L2 norm selection, R = random subsampling. Baseline = unpruned.\\\\n      SmolVLA collapses to 0% at all pruning levels on all suites.\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns: [Model, Suite, Baseline, \\\\\\\"k=0.95 (L2/R)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"k=0.9 (L2/R)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"k=0.7 (L2/R)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"k=0.5 (L2/R)\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: eval_pi05_*, eval_xvla_*, eval_openvla_*, eval_smolvla_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >\\\\n      Core evidence table covering all 4 models × key suites × 2 methods.\\\\n      Show Object + Goal for all models (2-axis illustration).\\\\n      Spatial/Long rows for π0.5 and X-VLA where data available.\\\\n      Bold best per cell. [TODO] cells for missing data.\\\\n      Full k-ratio curves in Appendix Table.\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_cv_prediction\\\\n    caption: >\\\\n      CV predicts method sensitivity. At k=0.95, low-CV π0.5 shows 15×\\\\n      larger method gap than high-CV models. At k=0.5, Δ(R−L2) scales\\\\n      monotonically with CV. Caveat: high-CV method-independence breaks\\\\n      at k≤0.5 for X-VLA.\\\\n    placement: appendix\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns: [Model, CV, \\\\\\\"k=0.95 Δ(R−L2)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"k=0.5 L2\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"k=0.5 R\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Δ(R−L2)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"k=0.7 L2\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"k=0.7 R\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Δ(R−L2)\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: eval_pi05_*, eval_xvla_*, eval_openvla_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >\\\\n      3 models ordered by CV. Key evidence for Claim 3 (D175 upgraded).\\\\n      k=0.95 column captures method-critical threshold finding.\\\\n      k=0.5 and k=0.7 columns capture aggressive-compression scaling law.\\\\n      May merge with fig_method_gap if space tight (figure shows k=0.95, table shows k=0.5/0.7).\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_vtr_results\\\\n    caption: >\\\\n      VTR recovery on SmolVLA (pixel-shuffle connector). Hard pruning yields\\\\n      0% SR; VTR recovers to near-baseline at all compression ratios.\\\\n      Mean ± std over 3 seeds.\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: ablation\\\\n    columns: [Architecture, M, Compression, \\\\\\\"SR (mean±std)\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: train_smolvla_sc_*, eval_smolvla_sc_*\\\\\\\"\\\\n    baseline_row: \\\\\\\"SmolVLA baseline (59.2%)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >\\\\n      Show: cross-attention M={16,32,64,128}, MLP adapter M=32.\\\\n      All within ±3pp of baseline. Flat M-curve is key finding.\\\\n      p=0.15 for cross-attention vs MLP at M=32.\\\\n      Cross-suite row: M=32 Spatial=53.2%, Goal=56.4%.\\\\n\\\\nliterature_strategy:\\\\n  citation_budget: 35\\\\n  macro_context:\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"VLA token efficiency and inference acceleration\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Vision connector design and token compression in VLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Task complexity and multi-step manipulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  micro_clusters:\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"VLA Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"vision language action model robotics 2024 2025 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"pi0 OpenVLA SmolVLA robot policy\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"black2024pi0\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"kim2024openvla\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"shukor2025smolvla\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"zitkovich2023rt2\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 6\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"VLM/LLM Token Efficiency\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"visual token pruning VLM 2024 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"VLA inference acceleration token reduction\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"chen2024fastv\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"hsu2025vlacache\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"wang2026vlaiap\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"yang2025efficientvla\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 8\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"ViT Token Pruning/Merging\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"vision transformer token pruning merging efficient inference 2021 2022 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"rao2021dynamicvit\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"bolya2023tome\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"liang2022evit\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 4\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"Vision Connectors & Token Compression\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"vision language model connector projection pixel shuffle\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Q-Former Perceiver Resampler cross-attention compression\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"li2023blip2\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"liu2023llava\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"shi2016subpixel\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 5\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"Closed-Loop Evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"LIBERO benchmark robotic manipulation lifelong learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"closed-loop evaluation robot simulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"liu2023libero\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 3\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"Negative Results & Evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"negative results machine learning evaluation pitfalls\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite: []\\\\n      target_count: 2\\\\n\\\\nwriting_order:\\\\n  - experiments\\\\n  - method\\\\n  - introduction\\\\n  - related_work\\\\n  - discussion\\\\n  - conclusion\\\\n  - abstract\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 19928, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016ygsN4b5fRsrvojGHZEWTk","session_id":"f3f75ff7-cbcf-4297-8f4a-a59d253e665c","uuid":"80006d06-616d-42cf-a23d-a854cd34b281","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:11:58.743Z"}
