{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ygsN4b5fRsrvojGHZEWTk","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have now read the full paper including the main text and all appendices. I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper's claims, methodology, experimental design, and results. Let me now compose the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: Vision Connectors and VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of how vision connector architecture determines the robustness of VLA models to post-connector token pruning. Six VLA architectures (450M--7B) across three connector families are evaluated on four LIBERO task suites under L2-norm and random pruning strategies. The main findings are: (1) token-independent connectors (linear, MLP) degrade gracefully while token-entangling connectors (pixel-shuffle, BART encoder) exhibit catastrophic or threshold collapse amplified by task complexity; (2) the coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms serves as a zero-cost diagnostic for pruning tolerance and method sensitivity; (3) a lightweight Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR, ~22M params) recovers SmolVLA from 0% to ~60% SR at 75% compression. A double dissociation between scoring method effectiveness and connector type (attention helps entangling connectors but not linear; L2 norm helps linear but not entangling) is reported.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Breadth of experimental coverage.** The study spans 6 architectures, 3 connector families, 4 task suites, multiple pruning strategies (L2, random, attention-based), and a wide range of retention ratios (k=0.3 to 0.95). This is genuinely the first cross-architecture, cross-task study of VLA token pruning robustness, filling an important gap.\n\n**S2. The double dissociation finding is compelling.** The result that attention-based scoring is effective on X-VLA but harmful on pi_0.5 Object, while L2-norm scoring shows the converse pattern, is well-supported by data from multiple suites and retention ratios. This is the paper's strongest result because it has immediate practical implications and is hard to dismiss as noise.\n\n**S3. Thorough SmolVLA cliff characterization.** The pixel-shuffle failure is rigorously established: multiple checkpoints (LoRA and official), multiple scoring methods (ACIS, L2, random), multiple mechanisms (zero-masking, true removal, ToMe), and all four LIBERO suites. Two independent baselines (44.8% and 59.2%) both show 0% under any pruning. This conclusively establishes the architectural cliff.\n\n**S4. VTR as a targeted solution with proper controls.** The flat M-curve (M=16 through M=128 within 1.1pp), the parameter-matched MLP adapter control (p=0.15), the M=128 passthrough demonstrating re-encoding rather than compression as the mechanism, and 3-seed validation all represent careful experimental design.\n\n**S5. Honest acknowledgment of limitations.** The paper explicitly flags the confounders (backbone, encoder, scale), the small sample for CV correlation (4 models), simulation-only evaluation, and the lack of causal isolation. The limitations paragraph in the experiments section and the appendix confounder analysis demonstrate intellectual honesty rare in this space.\n\n**S6. Non-monotonic phenomena are well-documented.** The pi_0.5 random cliff at k=0.85--0.925, the X-VLA k=0.85/k=0.80 inversion, and the pi_0.5 L2-norm V-shape are all multi-seed confirmed. The hypergeometric cliff model (Appendix) provides a clean mechanistic account for the random cliff phenomenon.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. CV as a \"diagnostic\" is severely underpowered.** The central claim that CV predicts pruning robustness rests on 4 closed-loop data points (pi_0.5, X-VLA, OpenVLA, SmolVLA). With n=4, any monotonic ordering of two variables will appear to correlate (Spearman rho is trivially high with 4 points). The paper calls this a \"scaling law\" in the framing documents and a \"diagnostic\" in the text, but neither characterization is statistically defensible. The CV range confounds with connector type: independent connectors are all low-CV, entangling are all high-CV. We cannot distinguish \"CV predicts robustness\" from \"connector type predicts robustness\" with this data. Adding more models (even from the same families) would be needed to validate CV as an independent predictor beyond connector type.\n\n**W2. The 2-axis framework (coupling x task complexity) lacks a clear testable boundary.** The paper claims token-entangling connectors are \"dramatically more sensitive\" to task complexity, but the evidence is mixed. X-VLA Long at k=0.95 drops 47pp, but pi_0.5 Long at k=0.95 also drops 30pp (91.6% to 61.2% random). The \"20x amplification\" claim (1.4pp Object vs 27.8pp Goal at k=0.9 for X-VLA) is computed from a single model at a single k value. The framework does not specify what the expected amplification factor should be for independent vs. entangling connectors, making it post-hoc descriptive rather than predictive.\n\n**W3. OpenVLA coverage is notably sparse.** OpenVLA is evaluated on only one suite (Object) in the main results table, with many cells marked \"--\". For a paper claiming a cross-architecture, cross-task analysis, having the only MLP-projector model evaluated on a single suite significantly weakens the framework. The k=0.95 random result for OpenVLA is missing from Table 2, and k=0.7 L2/Random data for OpenVLA is absent from the main table. The paper cannot make strong claims about how MLP connectors interact with task complexity without Goal/Long/Spatial data for OpenVLA.\n\n**W4. VTR universality is falsified but underexplored.** The paper reports that VTR on OpenVLA's MLP projector yields 0% SR (complete failure), but this is mentioned only in passing and relegated to the framing documents rather than appearing prominently in the paper. If VTR's mechanism is \"breaking rigid spatial entanglement\" as claimed, why does it actively harm MLP-projector models? The 0% result suggests severe overfitting, but the paper does not investigate why -- does cross-attention on MLP tokens destroy per-patch semantics? Is the training protocol (batch size 4, same hyperparameters) inappropriate for larger models? This is a significant missing experiment for a method paper.\n\n**W5. Seed and statistical rigor concerns.** Most conditions use a single seed. The paper acknowledges cross-seed Delta <= 5.8pp (Appendix), but this is a large variance for some of the claimed effects. OpenVLA L2 vs Random main-table comparisons mix seeds (L2: seed42, Random: seed7 for some k values). The \"scaling law\" table (Table in Appendix) uses mixed seeds for X-VLA (3-seed mean) and single seeds for pi_0.5 and OpenVLA, making the monotonic ordering partially an artifact of unequal statistical treatment.\n\n**W6. VTR baseline discrepancy.** VTR is trained with batch size 4 on 1 GPU, producing a 44.8% baseline, while the official SmolVLA checkpoint achieves 59.2%. The VTR results (60.1%) are compared against the 44.8% baseline for the \"exceeds baseline\" claim, but if compared against the 59.2% official baseline, VTR merely matches it. The paper acknowledges this but the framing in the main text could mislead: Table 5 lists \"Baseline (unpruned)\" as 44.8%, which is the reproduced baseline, not the official one. The key contribution should be framed as \"recovery from 0% to functional performance\" rather than \"exceeding baseline.\"\n\n**W7. No real-robot evaluation.** For a CoRL submission, the absence of any real-world validation is a significant gap. The paper's claims about \"deployment\" and \"practitioner\" implications rest entirely on LIBERO simulation. While LIBERO is a respected benchmark, VLA token pruning behavior could differ significantly under real-world visual conditions (lighting variation, background clutter, sensor noise).\n\n**W8. Attention-based scoring results for pi_0.5 raise methodological questions.** The attention scores are reported as having an extremely narrow range ([2e-6, 3.6e-3]) with \"systematic position bias favoring early tokens.\" This raises the question of whether the attention extraction protocol is appropriate for pi_0.5's architecture (PaliGemma backbone with different attention patterns than standard decoder-only LLMs). The paper does not discuss whether different attention extraction methods (e.g., gradient-based attention, attention from different layers) might produce different results.\n\n### 4. Questions to Authors\n\n**Q1.** The CV-robustness correlation has n=4 closed-loop points. Can you provide any evidence that CV is predictive *beyond* what connector type already tells us? For instance, within the MLP family (OpenVLA CV=0.215, OFT CV=0.219), is there any measurable difference in pruning robustness consistent with the 0.004 CV gap? Without such within-family evidence, CV is a proxy for connector type, not an independent predictor.\n\n**Q2.** The pi_0.5 random pruning cliff at k=0.85-0.925 is striking. You attribute it to RoPE position encoding disruption, but the X-VLA BART encoder (no RoPE) also shows non-monotonic behavior at similar k values (k=0.85: 67.9% < k=0.80: 89.0%). How do you reconcile the RoPE explanation with the presence of similar phenomena in non-RoPE architectures?\n\n**Q3.** For the VTR failure on OpenVLA (0% SR), did you attempt any of the following: (a) training only the VTR module while freezing OpenVLA weights, (b) using a different learning rate, (c) reducing the training steps to avoid overfitting? The failure mode (\"identical action every timestep\") suggests catastrophic overfitting rather than a fundamental architectural limitation. Can you provide training loss curves?\n\n**Q4.** Why is OpenVLA evaluated on only Object in the main results? The ROADMAP indicates OpenVLA Goal, Spatial, and Long experiments were planned or running. If data exists for additional suites, omitting them weakens the cross-task claims significantly.\n\n**Q5.** The Spearman correlation (rho=0.82, p=0.03 at k=0.5) mentioned in the summary -- can you clarify how this was computed? With only 3 models in the scaling law table, any monotonic ordering produces rho=1.0. Were additional data points included?\n\n**Q6.** Have you measured CV on any other VLA architectures (e.g., RoboFlamingo with Perceiver Resampler, which appears in the review context as a dropped experiment) to test whether the CV spectrum generalizes beyond the 6 models studied?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n**M1.** Table 2 main results: the SmolVLA Spatial k=0.95 entry shows 6.6% and 4.2%, but the abstract and introduction claim \"<=6.6% across all task suites and pruning methods.\" The Spatial k=0.95 result is technically non-zero and should be discussed as the exception, not subsumed under the \"0%\" characterization.\n\n**M2.** The paper title mentions \"Vision Connectors\" but the actual paper analyzes a broader set of factors (task complexity, scoring method, token count). The title could better reflect the multi-factor analysis.\n\n**M3.** Figure references assume all figures are generated, but several appear to reference PDFs that may not exist in the submission. Verify all figure files are included.\n\n**M4.** The \"20x amplification\" framing (1.4pp vs 27.8pp) is technically accurate but misleading: dividing two small differences is sensitive to the denominator. Absolute differences (1.4pp vs 27.8pp) are more informative.\n\n**M5.** Appendix Tables have inconsistent seed reporting. Some use seed42, some seed7, some multi-seed means. A clear seed convention table would help reproducibility.\n\n**M6.** The OFT model is included in the model table but contributes essentially nothing to the analysis beyond confirming OpenVLA's CV value. Consider whether it adds enough to justify the \"6 architectures\" claim.\n\n**M7.** The confounder analysis (Appendix G) is honest but could more explicitly state that the primary evidence for the connector's role is correlational, not causal.\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n- **Soundness:** 3/4. The experimental work is extensive and mostly rigorous, but the CV diagnostic claim is underpowered (n=4), some cross-seed comparisons are inconsistent, and the VTR universality failure is underexplored. The core observation that connector architecture matters is well-supported; the quantitative claims about CV as a predictor are not.\n\n- **Presentation:** 3/4. The paper is clearly written with good use of tables and figures. The main text is well-structured and the limitations are honestly stated. The appendix is thorough. However, the framing sometimes overreaches relative to the evidence (e.g., \"scaling law,\" \"diagnostic\"), and the mixed-seed reporting creates confusion.\n\n- **Contribution:** 3/4. This is a valuable empirical contribution to an important and timely topic. The cross-architecture analysis is genuinely novel and the double dissociation finding is strong. The practical implications (check your connector before deploying pruning) are clear and useful. However, the framework is more descriptive than predictive, and the method contribution (VTR) is limited to one connector type.\n\n- **Overall Score:** 6/10. The paper addresses an important gap: understanding why token pruning works on some VLAs but not others. The breadth of experiments and the double dissociation finding are genuine contributions. However, the central CV diagnostic claim is statistically underpowered, OpenVLA multi-suite data is incomplete, VTR universality is falsified without adequate investigation, and the absence of real-robot validation weakens a CoRL submission. The paper is above the acceptance threshold if the authors can address the OpenVLA suite coverage and clarify the CV diagnostic limitations, but in its current form it reads more as a thorough empirical study with useful observations than as a paper establishing a validated framework.\n\n- **Confidence:** 4/5. I am confident in my assessment. I have carefully read the full paper including all appendices. My main uncertainty is whether the missing OpenVLA cross-suite data (mentioned as running in the project files) will be included in the final submission, which could change my assessment of W3."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a888ab0de113d8e15 (use SendMessage with to: 'a888ab0de113d8e15' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 100145\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 157282</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3f75ff7-cbcf-4297-8f4a-a59d253e665c","uuid":"b0127b1d-c32c-4774-8141-ed5ecaf1edf3","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:13:57.794Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. \n\n## Your Review Scope\n- Experimental design quality (controls, baselines, metrics, statistical rigor)\n- Methodological soundness (VTR module design, pruning protocol, diagnostic metrics)\n- Reproducibility and completeness of results\n- Validity of claims vs. evidence provided\n\n## CoRL Review Standards\nCoRL is the Conference on Robot Learning. Rating scale 1-10:\n- 10: Top 5% of all papers, seminal contribution\n- 8-9: Strong accept, clear contribution, well-executed\n- 6-7: Weak accept, interesting but with notable gaps\n- 5: Borderline, contributions don't clearly outweigh weaknesses\n- 3-4: Weak reject, significant issues\n- 1-2: Strong reject\n\nAcceptance rate ~30-35%. Papers must demonstrate relevance to robot learning.\n\n## The Paper\n\n**Title**: \"How Vision Connectors Shape Token Pruning Robustness in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n**Summary**: This paper presents a systematic cross-architecture analysis of token pruning in VLAs (5 models, 3 connector families, 4 LIBERO task suites). Key findings:\n1. A 2-axis framework: connector architecture (token coupling) is the primary determinant of pruning robustness; task complexity modulates for token-entangling connectors.\n2. Pixel-shuffle connectors yield 0% SR at k=0.95 (catastrophic)\n3. MLP projectors show graded degradation\n4. Linear projections tolerate up to 90% removal\n5. CV (Coefficient of Variation) of token importance predicts both pruning tolerance and optimal strategy (Spearman ρ=0.82, p=0.03 at k=0.5)\n6. VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding, 22M params) recovers pixel-shuffle from 0% to ~60% baseline at any compression ratio\n7. VTR fails on MLP projectors (0% SR) — connector-dependent\n\n**Models studied**: π0 (3.3B, linear proj, 256 tokens, CV=0.038), π0.5 (4B, linear proj, 768 tokens, CV=0.055), X-VLA (880M, BART 12L encoder, 50 tokens, CV=0.151), OpenVLA (7B, 3-layer MLP, 256 tokens, CV=0.215), OFT (7B, 3-layer MLP, 512 tokens, CV=0.219), SmolVLA (450M, pixel-shuffle, 128 tokens, CV=0.314)\n\n**Key Tables**:\n- Table 1: Model overview with CV and entropy\n- Table 2: X-VLA cross-suite pruning at k=0.9 (Object -1.6pp, Long -66.5pp)\n- Table 3: π0.5 random pruning cross-suite (Object cliff at k=0.85-0.925)\n- Table 4: X-VLA cliff zone 3-seed (k=0.85: 67.9%±0.31pp, k=0.80: 89.0%±0.72pp)\n- Table 5: CV scaling law — L2norm vs random gap monotonic at k=0.5/0.7, breaks at k=0.3\n- Table 6: Spearman correlation (k=0.5: ρ=0.82, p=0.03; k=0.3: ρ=0.45, p=0.15)\n- Table 7: Cross-suite L2norm vs random gap at k=0.5\n- Table 8: Attention-based pruning at k=0.5 (X-VLA attn-last Object 99.2%)\n- Table 9: VTR M-sweep (M=16-128 all within ±5pp of 59.2% baseline)\n- Table 10: VTR cross-suite recovery (mean recovery ratio 0.96)\n- Table 11: VTR connector-dependent (SmolVLA works, OpenVLA 0%)\n- Table 12: π0.5 L2-norm pruning cross-suite (Appendix)\n\n**Experimental design reference (from review_context.yaml)** — the paper should cover these imagined experiments:\n1. π0.5 4-suite pruning characterization\n2. Cross-suite framework validation (5 models × 4 suites, framework accuracy 85%)\n3. VTR SmolVLA cross-suite recovery (M=32, recovery ratio 0.96)\n4. VTR/VTC on OpenVLA — universality falsification (all fail, 0% SR)\n5. OpenVLA VTC M-sweep (M=32/64/96, all failed)\n6. OpenVLA-OFT cross-suite pruning (blocked by GPU, only CV/entropy analysis)\n7. OFT token importance CV measurement (CV=0.219)\n8. X-VLA k=0.8/k=0.85 3-seed cliff localization\n9. RoboFlamingo Perceiver Resampler (training killed, dropped from paper)\n10. E5 attention-based pruning cross-suite\n11. CV scaling law Spearman correlation analysis\n12. SmolVLA VTR M-sweep 3-seed + architecture control\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on experimental/methodological strengths)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on experimental/methodological weaknesses)\n4. **Questions to Authors** (specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues** \n6. **Scores** (provide these exactly):\n   - Soundness: 1-4\n   - Presentation: 1-4\n   - Contribution: 1-4\n   - Overall Score: 1-10\n   - Confidence: 1-5\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Focus on what experiments are missing or weak, statistical validity, and whether claims are properly supported by evidence.","agentId":"a888ab0de113d8e15","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have now read the full paper including the main text and all appendices. I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper's claims, methodology, experimental design, and results. Let me now compose the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: Vision Connectors and VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of how vision connector architecture determines the robustness of VLA models to post-connector token pruning. Six VLA architectures (450M--7B) across three connector families are evaluated on four LIBERO task suites under L2-norm and random pruning strategies. The main findings are: (1) token-independent connectors (linear, MLP) degrade gracefully while token-entangling connectors (pixel-shuffle, BART encoder) exhibit catastrophic or threshold collapse amplified by task complexity; (2) the coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms serves as a zero-cost diagnostic for pruning tolerance and method sensitivity; (3) a lightweight Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR, ~22M params) recovers SmolVLA from 0% to ~60% SR at 75% compression. A double dissociation between scoring method effectiveness and connector type (attention helps entangling connectors but not linear; L2 norm helps linear but not entangling) is reported.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Breadth of experimental coverage.** The study spans 6 architectures, 3 connector families, 4 task suites, multiple pruning strategies (L2, random, attention-based), and a wide range of retention ratios (k=0.3 to 0.95). This is genuinely the first cross-architecture, cross-task study of VLA token pruning robustness, filling an important gap.\n\n**S2. The double dissociation finding is compelling.** The result that attention-based scoring is effective on X-VLA but harmful on pi_0.5 Object, while L2-norm scoring shows the converse pattern, is well-supported by data from multiple suites and retention ratios. This is the paper's strongest result because it has immediate practical implications and is hard to dismiss as noise.\n\n**S3. Thorough SmolVLA cliff characterization.** The pixel-shuffle failure is rigorously established: multiple checkpoints (LoRA and official), multiple scoring methods (ACIS, L2, random), multiple mechanisms (zero-masking, true removal, ToMe), and all four LIBERO suites. Two independent baselines (44.8% and 59.2%) both show 0% under any pruning. This conclusively establishes the architectural cliff.\n\n**S4. VTR as a targeted solution with proper controls.** The flat M-curve (M=16 through M=128 within 1.1pp), the parameter-matched MLP adapter control (p=0.15), the M=128 passthrough demonstrating re-encoding rather than compression as the mechanism, and 3-seed validation all represent careful experimental design.\n\n**S5. Honest acknowledgment of limitations.** The paper explicitly flags the confounders (backbone, encoder, scale), the small sample for CV correlation (4 models), simulation-only evaluation, and the lack of causal isolation. The limitations paragraph in the experiments section and the appendix confounder analysis demonstrate intellectual honesty rare in this space.\n\n**S6. Non-monotonic phenomena are well-documented.** The pi_0.5 random cliff at k=0.85--0.925, the X-VLA k=0.85/k=0.80 inversion, and the pi_0.5 L2-norm V-shape are all multi-seed confirmed. The hypergeometric cliff model (Appendix) provides a clean mechanistic account for the random cliff phenomenon.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. CV as a \"diagnostic\" is severely underpowered.** The central claim that CV predicts pruning robustness rests on 4 closed-loop data points (pi_0.5, X-VLA, OpenVLA, SmolVLA). With n=4, any monotonic ordering of two variables will appear to correlate (Spearman rho is trivially high with 4 points). The paper calls this a \"scaling law\" in the framing documents and a \"diagnostic\" in the text, but neither characterization is statistically defensible. The CV range confounds with connector type: independent connectors are all low-CV, entangling are all high-CV. We cannot distinguish \"CV predicts robustness\" from \"connector type predicts robustness\" with this data. Adding more models (even from the same families) would be needed to validate CV as an independent predictor beyond connector type.\n\n**W2. The 2-axis framework (coupling x task complexity) lacks a clear testable boundary.** The paper claims token-entangling connectors are \"dramatically more sensitive\" to task complexity, but the evidence is mixed. X-VLA Long at k=0.95 drops 47pp, but pi_0.5 Long at k=0.95 also drops 30pp (91.6% to 61.2% random). The \"20x amplification\" claim (1.4pp Object vs 27.8pp Goal at k=0.9 for X-VLA) is computed from a single model at a single k value. The framework does not specify what the expected amplification factor should be for independent vs. entangling connectors, making it post-hoc descriptive rather than predictive.\n\n**W3. OpenVLA coverage is notably sparse.** OpenVLA is evaluated on only one suite (Object) in the main results table, with many cells marked \"--\". For a paper claiming a cross-architecture, cross-task analysis, having the only MLP-projector model evaluated on a single suite significantly weakens the framework. The k=0.95 random result for OpenVLA is missing from Table 2, and k=0.7 L2/Random data for OpenVLA is absent from the main table. The paper cannot make strong claims about how MLP connectors interact with task complexity without Goal/Long/Spatial data for OpenVLA.\n\n**W4. VTR universality is falsified but underexplored.** The paper reports that VTR on OpenVLA's MLP projector yields 0% SR (complete failure), but this is mentioned only in passing and relegated to the framing documents rather than appearing prominently in the paper. If VTR's mechanism is \"breaking rigid spatial entanglement\" as claimed, why does it actively harm MLP-projector models? The 0% result suggests severe overfitting, but the paper does not investigate why -- does cross-attention on MLP tokens destroy per-patch semantics? Is the training protocol (batch size 4, same hyperparameters) inappropriate for larger models? This is a significant missing experiment for a method paper.\n\n**W5. Seed and statistical rigor concerns.** Most conditions use a single seed. The paper acknowledges cross-seed Delta <= 5.8pp (Appendix), but this is a large variance for some of the claimed effects. OpenVLA L2 vs Random main-table comparisons mix seeds (L2: seed42, Random: seed7 for some k values). The \"scaling law\" table (Table in Appendix) uses mixed seeds for X-VLA (3-seed mean) and single seeds for pi_0.5 and OpenVLA, making the monotonic ordering partially an artifact of unequal statistical treatment.\n\n**W6. VTR baseline discrepancy.** VTR is trained with batch size 4 on 1 GPU, producing a 44.8% baseline, while the official SmolVLA checkpoint achieves 59.2%. The VTR results (60.1%) are compared against the 44.8% baseline for the \"exceeds baseline\" claim, but if compared against the 59.2% official baseline, VTR merely matches it. The paper acknowledges this but the framing in the main text could mislead: Table 5 lists \"Baseline (unpruned)\" as 44.8%, which is the reproduced baseline, not the official one. The key contribution should be framed as \"recovery from 0% to functional performance\" rather than \"exceeding baseline.\"\n\n**W7. No real-robot evaluation.** For a CoRL submission, the absence of any real-world validation is a significant gap. The paper's claims about \"deployment\" and \"practitioner\" implications rest entirely on LIBERO simulation. While LIBERO is a respected benchmark, VLA token pruning behavior could differ significantly under real-world visual conditions (lighting variation, background clutter, sensor noise).\n\n**W8. Attention-based scoring results for pi_0.5 raise methodological questions.** The attention scores are reported as having an extremely narrow range ([2e-6, 3.6e-3]) with \"systematic position bias favoring early tokens.\" This raises the question of whether the attention extraction protocol is appropriate for pi_0.5's architecture (PaliGemma backbone with different attention patterns than standard decoder-only LLMs). The paper does not discuss whether different attention extraction methods (e.g., gradient-based attention, attention from different layers) might produce different results.\n\n### 4. Questions to Authors\n\n**Q1.** The CV-robustness correlation has n=4 closed-loop points. Can you provide any evidence that CV is predictive *beyond* what connector type already tells us? For instance, within the MLP family (OpenVLA CV=0.215, OFT CV=0.219), is there any measurable difference in pruning robustness consistent with the 0.004 CV gap? Without such within-family evidence, CV is a proxy for connector type, not an independent predictor.\n\n**Q2.** The pi_0.5 random pruning cliff at k=0.85-0.925 is striking. You attribute it to RoPE position encoding disruption, but the X-VLA BART encoder (no RoPE) also shows non-monotonic behavior at similar k values (k=0.85: 67.9% < k=0.80: 89.0%). How do you reconcile the RoPE explanation with the presence of similar phenomena in non-RoPE architectures?\n\n**Q3.** For the VTR failure on OpenVLA (0% SR), did you attempt any of the following: (a) training only the VTR module while freezing OpenVLA weights, (b) using a different learning rate, (c) reducing the training steps to avoid overfitting? The failure mode (\"identical action every timestep\") suggests catastrophic overfitting rather than a fundamental architectural limitation. Can you provide training loss curves?\n\n**Q4.** Why is OpenVLA evaluated on only Object in the main results? The ROADMAP indicates OpenVLA Goal, Spatial, and Long experiments were planned or running. If data exists for additional suites, omitting them weakens the cross-task claims significantly.\n\n**Q5.** The Spearman correlation (rho=0.82, p=0.03 at k=0.5) mentioned in the summary -- can you clarify how this was computed? With only 3 models in the scaling law table, any monotonic ordering produces rho=1.0. Were additional data points included?\n\n**Q6.** Have you measured CV on any other VLA architectures (e.g., RoboFlamingo with Perceiver Resampler, which appears in the review context as a dropped experiment) to test whether the CV spectrum generalizes beyond the 6 models studied?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n**M1.** Table 2 main results: the SmolVLA Spatial k=0.95 entry shows 6.6% and 4.2%, but the abstract and introduction claim \"<=6.6% across all task suites and pruning methods.\" The Spatial k=0.95 result is technically non-zero and should be discussed as the exception, not subsumed under the \"0%\" characterization.\n\n**M2.** The paper title mentions \"Vision Connectors\" but the actual paper analyzes a broader set of factors (task complexity, scoring method, token count). The title could better reflect the multi-factor analysis.\n\n**M3.** Figure references assume all figures are generated, but several appear to reference PDFs that may not exist in the submission. Verify all figure files are included.\n\n**M4.** The \"20x amplification\" framing (1.4pp vs 27.8pp) is technically accurate but misleading: dividing two small differences is sensitive to the denominator. Absolute differences (1.4pp vs 27.8pp) are more informative.\n\n**M5.** Appendix Tables have inconsistent seed reporting. Some use seed42, some seed7, some multi-seed means. A clear seed convention table would help reproducibility.\n\n**M6.** The OFT model is included in the model table but contributes essentially nothing to the analysis beyond confirming OpenVLA's CV value. Consider whether it adds enough to justify the \"6 architectures\" claim.\n\n**M7.** The confounder analysis (Appendix G) is honest but could more explicitly state that the primary evidence for the connector's role is correlational, not causal.\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n- **Soundness:** 3/4. The experimental work is extensive and mostly rigorous, but the CV diagnostic claim is underpowered (n=4), some cross-seed comparisons are inconsistent, and the VTR universality failure is underexplored. The core observation that connector architecture matters is well-supported; the quantitative claims about CV as a predictor are not.\n\n- **Presentation:** 3/4. The paper is clearly written with good use of tables and figures. The main text is well-structured and the limitations are honestly stated. The appendix is thorough. However, the framing sometimes overreaches relative to the evidence (e.g., \"scaling law,\" \"diagnostic\"), and the mixed-seed reporting creates confusion.\n\n- **Contribution:** 3/4. This is a valuable empirical contribution to an important and timely topic. The cross-architecture analysis is genuinely novel and the double dissociation finding is strong. The practical implications (check your connector before deploying pruning) are clear and useful. However, the framework is more descriptive than predictive, and the method contribution (VTR) is limited to one connector type.\n\n- **Overall Score:** 6/10. The paper addresses an important gap: understanding why token pruning works on some VLAs but not others. The breadth of experiments and the double dissociation finding are genuine contributions. However, the central CV diagnostic claim is statistically underpowered, OpenVLA multi-suite data is incomplete, VTR universality is falsified without adequate investigation, and the absence of real-robot validation weakens a CoRL submission. The paper is above the acceptance threshold if the authors can address the OpenVLA suite coverage and clarify the CV diagnostic limitations, but in its current form it reads more as a thorough empirical study with useful observations than as a paper establishing a validated framework.\n\n- **Confidence:** 4/5. I am confident in my assessment. I have carefully read the full paper including all appendices. My main uncertainty is whether the missing OpenVLA cross-suite data (mentioned as running in the project files) will be included in the final submission, which could change my assessment of W3."}],"totalDurationMs":157282,"totalTokens":100145,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24660,"cache_read_input_tokens":71941,"output_tokens":3543,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":24660},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3543,"cache_read_input_tokens":71941,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24660,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":24660,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
